Z||000010||LEE498||05-16-99||09:56 PM||||Thank's for the info PHIL. :^)
Z||000011||Shaun41178||05-17-99||08:01 PM||||Well according to the numbers for the cam given above that will equate to .421 intake and .444 exhaust lift with the use of 1.5 rockers.  these are pretty good numbers to make around 200 hp.<p>However I would suggest a different cam for you Lee since you will be running nitrous.  nitrous engines like lots of exhaust lift and exhasut duration.  GM cam part # 12363220 would be a better cam I think.  It has .427 intake and .454 exhaust with 204/216 degrres of duration and a lobe separation angle of 107 degrees.  This tight lobe separation should give a nice smooth idle.  Plus it has more exhaust lift then the melling cam and 2 degrees more exhaust duration.<p>I am ordering this cam as we speak.  Getting a pretty good deal on it too.  $99  If you know someone who works in a Gm dealership parts department you can get it cheap too.  I will be installing it in my engien within the next month and expect a significant increase in hp especially with my nitrous.  Also have the matching springs retainers and keys along with pushrods coming too.  all these parts should cost me around $200 with my "special discount"<p>Your welcome Lee for the piston part number.<p>200 hp is more then obtainalbe from a 3.2.  I have a little more then 130 at the wheels right now in my 2.8 which I am guessing is around 150-160 at the crank.  With new cam and other small thingies i expect to be around 150-160 at the wheels.  Which would equate to around 180-190 at the crank.<p>Lee also make sure that everything is clean now since you had to shave off part of the block.  You don't want little metal shavings finding there way between the bearing surfaces.  Get it super clean.  Pull it apart again if you have to to make sure that everything is clean and dirt/metal shavings free.  Good luck with the rest of your rebuild.
Z||000012||LEE498||05-17-99||09:53 PM||||Thank's a million SHAUN, you've really helped me alot man.I'm going to order that cam TOMORROW.Your right nitrous loves exhaust.I was a little worried about valve clearance and dependability with the high lift. I've cleaned that block about 6 times, it should be OK well shoot! you got me worried now,my heads won't be back for 2-weeks anyway i'll pull it back down and clean it one last good time just to be safe. Thanks again SHAUN! You guys aught to check out SHAUN'S web page it got a lot of good stuff in it!  
Z||000013||Phil||05-18-99||05:01 AM||||Just to set the record striaght, I'm using 1.6 roller rockers
Z||000014||HF6582||05-18-99||04:58 PM||||Lee,<br>when you put the heads back on use ARP head<br>STUDs, they clamp the heads to the block better than using bolts.<br>they are a little pricy but not to bad.<br>I'll have to look the ARP number up for you.<p>Scott
Z||000015||LEE498||05-18-99||07:20 PM||||Studs aren't a bad ideal, but i'm getting a little low on bread...Ihave them on my 84 t-bird a very hot street car,..6.40's at 110 mph I'm a 'FORD' man, but this little fieros has seriously got me hooked.!! But any way i went to order that cam from the GM dealership they acted like they didn't know what they where doing, i give them the part number and he told me it will be 4-6 weeks before it gets in he didn't even check his computer to see where it had to come from. He kinda made me feel like i was wasting his time wth my little v6, by that time i was i little pissed and i just left.But i did go to ADVANCED AUTO and i ordered a SPEED-PRO cam.part # is CS192R it has big lift 443 intake 465 exhaust duration at 0.50 214-224 power range 2000-4500 and it has a good idle too.Only costed me $115 cheaper than what I was expecting too. I called the FIERO STOREs tech line they said i'll be alright with the valve clearance,but i'll double check any way.IT'll be here TOMORROW too.Advanced auto's got a large selection of cam's for the 2.8 and 3.1.     
Z||000016||LEE498||05-18-99||07:25 PM||||THAT TIME IS 1/8 MILE TIME!!!
Z||000017||Shaun41178||05-18-99||09:52 PM||||Lee you might not want to hear this but I think it is in your best interest since you are builing a hi-po motor and plan on using nitrous.<p>First of all I have to ask, how many times have you unbolted the rods from the rod caps?  I hope not too many times.  I have been told that you can only retighten rod bolts once after they have been torqued for the first time.  This is because the rod bolt stretches and if stretched too many times they become weak.  I think it is if you have to torque the rod bolts more then twice then they should be replaced.  I am sure you don't want a rod bolt breaking at 5000 rpm and destroying your brand new engine.<p>Rod bolts undergo tremendous amounts of stress.  Now this is what i have been told.  Just for arguments sake I say you visit a local machine shop or two and get several different opinions.  This statement I made may be true but I am unsure as to exactly how many times they can be torqued.<p>Did you go to a pontiac dealership to purchase that cam?  Not that it matters but I am going through a buick dealership.  As long as it is GM it doesn't matter.  I have never really had good luck with my local pontiac dealership.  Lots of run around and when I say Fiero they kind of wonder as to why I would want to buy parts for that car.<p>4-6 weeks huh?  I hope it doesn't take that long for mine.  I think those guys were giving you the run around.  They can get parts to fix a car within one day.  I don't see how it could take longer for a cam then for a headlamp lets say when everything comes from the same place.
Z||000018||Shaun41178||05-18-99||09:56 PM||||Oh yea Lee I hope you made sure that the cam you purchased is a "computer friendly" cam.<p>The cam that I getting, well there is another one with the exact same lift and duration made by GM except it is not computer friendly.  I don't know why but I think it has something to do with the lobe separation angle making the idle lopey which will cause the computer to go haywire.
Z||000019||Eric||05-19-99||01:40 PM||||Shaun,<br>Is that GM cam good for a turbo too?  I am going to do the stroker thing for sure, but I don't think I'll be satisfied with only 200 horsepower.  I have been debating between nitrous and turbo as supplimental forms of power (about 50-70 extra).  Both are affordable, but nitrous only works when you have the bottle on. And from what I have seen, the nitrous power curve comes on much harder than the turbo.  <p>Another problem with nitrous is the constant bottle refilling.  How often do you have to get it refilled and how much does each refill cost?<p> 
Z||000020||Eric||05-19-99||01:46 PM||||Lee, <br>Those pistons you mentioned, part#H562P, are Speed Pro pistons, right?  
Z||000021||LEE498||05-19-99||03:39 PM||||Eric,they'er Federal-Mogul.My opion nitrous is the best cheap horse power there is.But a turbo is better, if you can get it right.You know,fuel,timing,boost-retard,waste-gate.Basicly i think it's very expenive to get one, and get it set up to compete with nitrous.But if you got the CASH then the turbo will be very hard to beat.NOS-$560+$40 each refill 10 lb bottle gets about 8-12 full 1/4 mile passes.But on the street just playing around it should last for a while,shooting 50hp.TURBO $1500-$4000 plus the install$$$$$$ unless you do it your self and i believe it's a pain in the arss! But in the long run with the nos refills it'll be the same. Thats a LOONG RUN!      
Z||000022||Eric||05-19-99||04:59 PM||||Nitrous is "easier" horsepower because there is no fabrication involved.  Buying a turbo kit is out of the question, $4000.  However, the parts nessessary for the turbo, excluding the exaust and intake work, can be found on a lot of older turbo cars.  I'm not ready to do anything but my rebuild right now.  I was just thinking I might want more power in the future and by the sound of things, nitrous is the way to get it.  Who knows, maybe I'll want to take the extra time next winter and install a turbo.  After all, the winters in Ohio keep me off the road for weeks at a time.<p>I'm not much of a drag racer, so I won't be taking my Fiero to the strip much.  I don't think I would open up the blue bottle for autocross either and turbo lag can kick you're butt on a tight little parking lot course.  I just don't know.
Z||000023||Eric||05-19-99||09:14 PM||||Lee, <br>Are you worried about that Speed Pro cam being too much for the computer to handle?  If it works, it should make some good power.
Z||000024||Shaun41178||05-20-99||12:14 AM||||Lee that cam can be used with a turbo.  Just about any cam can be used with a turbo but some cams make more with forced induction then others.  That cam should work fine.  However GM does make a cam that is recommended for turbocharged engines.  That cam part number is 12353919.  it has 204/214 duration and 420/442 with a lobe centerline of 112.  and it says it is good to 6500 rpm with the proper valve springs and lifters.  Lifters would probably need to be some type of reduced pump up style.<p>As far as filing my bottle, I fill it about every 1-1.5 months.  I have never been to a track with it.  I mostly use it on the street in short bursts.  Sometimes longer then 1.5 months.  Refills around here range from $3 to $3.50 per pound.  I usually pay $3.50 cause the one that charges $3 is an hour away so it would cost more in gas to drive there and back so I drive the short distance and pay more.  The most it has ever cost me to fill is $35.  It usually runs me around $23 though cause I never let it get completly full.<p>Eric what part of ohio?  I live in Toledo.<p>
Z||000025||Eric||05-20-99||03:12 PM||||I'm from Columbus, Shaun.  I suppose winters are even worse for you, being up there in Toledo.  I became scared of winter driving very quickly when I drove my Fiero in less than 1/2 inch of snow and kept swaping ends under braking.<p>What kinds of places sell nitrous refills?  I'd have to assume Jeg's would sell nitrous, since they sell pretty much everything that has to do with drag racing.
